<!--
  Robot configuration for the OctoQuad turret-encoder bench rig.

  THIS IS NOT A ROBOT. It is a spare Control Hub on a bench with a Digital
  Chicken Labs OctoQuad (FTC Edition) on I2C and the turret's two Studica
  Cypher Max encoders, their absolute (PWM) outputs on the OctoQuad's
  pulse-width channels. It lets Bench: OctoQuad measure each encoder's pulse
  range and direction without a robot (#169).

  Like the robot configs, it ships inside the APK and appears in the Driver
  Station list as "robot_octoquad_rig" as soon as the app is installed. The one
  manual step, once per hub: on the rig's Driver Station,
  Configure Robot -> robot_octoquad_rig -> Activate. The Bench: OctoQuad OpMode
  warns if a configuration without an OctoQuad is active.

  WIRING:
  OctoQuad on the Control Hub's I2C bus 3, the same bus the competition robots
  use for it, so the bench facts carry over. Encoder A's absolute output on
  OctoQuad channel 4, encoder B's on channel 5 (TurretSubsystem.ABS_CHANNEL_A
  / _B); each Cypher Max's voltage switch on 3V3, since the OctoQuad is not 5 V
  tolerant.

  Device names here must match org.firstinspires.ftc.teamcode.hardware.DeviceNames
  (the OCTOQUAD_RIG list). RobotConfigXmlTest fails the build if they disagree.
  For I2C, `bus` is what the SDK reads; `port` is kept equal to it.
-->
<Robot type="FirstInspires-FTC">
    <LynxUsbDevice name="Control Hub Portal" serialNumber="(embedded)" parentModuleAddress="173">
        <LynxModule name="Control Hub" port="173">

            <!-- Turret angle sensing under test: OctoQuad on I2C bus 3. -->
            <OctoQuadFTC name="octoquad" port="3" bus="3" />

        </LynxModule>
    </LynxUsbDevice>
</Robot>
